I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the supplied diffraction-analysis guidance first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build/refine them in GSAS-II, and verify that every submitted file is reproducible from the final project.

The chemistry points to manganese–tungsten oxides, but at 500 °C the scan could contain MnWO₄, unreacted WO₃/Mn₂O₃, or intermediate Mn–W–O phases. I’m treating phase count as an inference problem and will use the full profile—not precursor plausibility alone—to distinguish them.

Peak positions now give a coherent first model: triclinic WO₃ dominates, while the non-WO₃ lines near 33.3, 38.5, 45.1, 49.6, 55.1, and 66.0° are consistent with α‑Mn₂O₃. MnWO₄’s diagnostic 15.5/18.5/30.0° peaks are absent. I’m moving to whole-pattern refinement to test whether those two precursor phases fully explain the scan and to discriminate the closely related WO₃ and Mn₂O₃ reference models.

The first full-profile trial is still refining; the 8,301-point scan and low-symmetry WO₃ model make each profile iteration relatively expensive. I’m retaining intermediate projects so a numerically lower residual can be checked against physically sensible cells, phase scales, and peak-shape terms.

The physical bounds exposed an important issue: the first triclinic reference only reached ~28.6% Rwp when it could no longer compensate with an implausible 7.3 mm specimen displacement. That means its apparent 11% fit was not trustworthy. I’m comparing the alternative low-temperature WO₃ settings under the same constraints before adding Mn₂O₃.

The cubic Mn₂O₃ trial did not converge to a useful mixture (36.8% Rwp), so I’m not accepting its nominal 39% fraction. The orthorhombic α‑Mn₂O₃ and conventional monoclinic WO₃ refinements are still running; those are the structurally better-founded alternatives.

The two-phase model is now chemically and crystallographically coherent: adding orthorhombic α‑Mn₂O₃ drops Rwp from 10.75% to 6.51%, with an initial crystalline fraction near 71.5% WO₃ / 28.5% Mn₂O₃. Remaining residuals are mostly positive/negative peak pairs, indicating that the WO₃ lattice symmetry or anisotropic broadening—not another obvious chemistry—is still imperfect. I’m testing whether the lower-symmetry triclinic WO₃ model resolves those split positions.

The triclinic competition test is computationally heavier because it adds six independent cell metrics and hundreds of overlapping reflections. The key acceptance criterion is whether it takes substantial WO₃ scale while lowering Rwp; a tiny scale or no improvement will rule it out as overfitting.

The lower-symmetry test converged to a nearly monoclinic metric and did not beat the two-phase monoclinic fit, so the apparent extra splitting is not compelling evidence for a separate WO₃ polymorph. I’m now testing the specimen effects that change intensities and widths—preferred orientation, surface roughness, and anisotropic strain—before refining atomic coordinates.

The stable replay model gives 7.80% Rwp and repeats unchanged on rerun. Preferred orientation is negligible (March ratios remain within ~2% of 1). Physically plausible refinements favor polarization ≈0.82, stronger Lorentzian broadening, and phase-dependent strain/size; unconstrained surface roughness and Caglioti terms run to nonphysical values, so I’m excluding those.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to monoclinic WO₃ plus orthorhombic α‑Mn₂O₃, with the crystalline split near 72:28. I’m now completing the coupled refinement and checking that the saved GSAS-II project reproduces the exported CIFs, fractions, and profile exactly.

The combined profile model is stable at Rwp 7.26% and shifts the crystalline fraction to 73.4% WO₃ / 26.6% Mn₂O₃. The remaining strongest mismatch is intensity rather than peak position, so I’m testing only small, symmetry-preserving coordinate adjustments against the literature starting structures; I’ll retain them only if the geometry and replay check remain sound.

